        <h2>Summary / Description</h2>
        <div class="description">
          The third flight of the Gemini program set a new duration record, at almost 8 full days.  The primary objective of Gemini 5 was to practice and test the orbital rendezvous and docking capabilities of the spacecraft using a new guidance and navigation system and to assess the ability of humans to remain in space for long durations.  The crew, Commander Gordon Cooper, Jr. and Pilot Charles Conrad, also carried out 17 science experiments, five of which studied the physiological processes of the human body as it adapts to microgravity.<P>Continuous, real-time physiological information on all Gemini crewmembers was monitored via telemetered data and voice transmissions.  Data was provided by a two-lead electrocardiogram, an impedance pneumograph measuring respiration, an oral thermistor measuring body temperature, and a blood pressure measuring system.  All of this hardware, including signal conditioners, was mounted on a biosensor harness that fit under the astronauts' flight suits.  Additionally, some inflight film footage was utilized for postflight assessment of crew health.<P>Six human life sciences experiments studying various physiological aspects of prolonged space flight were also successfully completed on Gemini 5.  The Cardiovascular Reflex Conditioning Experiment investigated the effectiveness of the pneumatic cuff technique in preventing orthostatic intolerance, a condition of low blood pressure after space flight caused by the adaptive body fluid loss in microgravity.  The inflatable leg cuffs worn the first four days of the mission by the pilot pressurized automatically every two minutes out of six in order to stimulate blood movement within the body.  In fact, the pilot?s pulse rate returned to normal more quickly, and he lost 4 percent less plasma volume than did the command pilot.<P>The Inflight Exerciser experiment not only allowed the astronauts to exercise in the constrained space of the Gemini capsule, but also measured the degree of cardiovascular adaptation or deconditioning of the human body during prolonged space flight by recording the performance and rate of recovery from exercise periods.  Another experiment used an inflight phonocardiogram to record astronauts? heart sounds in order to provide some insight into the functional cardiac status of astronauts during space flights.  The Bone Demineralization study investigated the occurrence and degree of any bone demineralization (loss of essential minerals like calcium from the bones) resulting from space flight to determine if corrective measures would be necessary during long duration space flight.  The Human Otolith experiment studied the ability of the astronauts to estimate horizontality with reference to the spacecraft in the absence of vision and primary gravitational cues, as well as the possible effect of prolonged weightlessness on otolith  function (an organ of the inner ear responsible for maintaining balance and orientation).  The Visual Acuity investigation studied the affects of space flight on vision.<P>After the flight, no detrimental medical changes were noted in the astronauts that had not been observed earlier, except that both astronauts lost more calcium from their bones on this longer duration mission. Because there were only two crewmembers providing data on physiologic changes, no definite medical conclusions resulted from the flight.  In fact, the medical observations and experiments served to instigate further research into human space adaptation and proved to be just the beginning of the quest for knowledge about space physiology. <P>The success of the Gemini 5 flight was measured on many levels.  Being the first medium duration flight, it provided scientists and engineers with much data and proved that humans were capable of remaining productive and healthy on extended stays in space.<END>
